::ENG3_U06_L05_Q01::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A presentation argues that setting affects a character's values; the student can choose media showing the setting, such as a labeled image of the town square where the conflict begins.
Which multimedia element best supports that purpose?{
~A. A decorative slide transition#Incorrect. A transition does not explain the setting or the character's values. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. A labeled image of the setting with a caption tied to the claim#Correct. The image and caption help the audience connect setting to the presentation's claim. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. A sound effect after every bullet point#Incorrect. Sound effects can distract from the evidence and analysis. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. A title font that looks old-fashioned#Incorrect. Font choice may affect style, but it does not give the audience useful evidence.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q02::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student includes a photograph of a storm-damaged library beside a claim about community recovery, but the slide has no caption explaining the connection.
What is the most useful revision?{
~A. Make the photograph larger#Incorrect. Size alone does not explain the photograph's purpose. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Put the photograph on a separate slide with no words#Incorrect. This still leaves the audience without a connection to the claim. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Add three more photographs from the same source#Incorrect. More media does not automatically improve understanding. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. Add a caption explaining how the photograph clarifies the evidence#Correct. A caption helps the audience understand why the image belongs in the presentation. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q03::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student needs to explain changes over time in a research presentation.
Which media choice is strongest?{
=A. A timeline that labels the most important dates#Correct. A timeline helps the audience understand sequence and focus on the most relevant evidence. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. A collage of unrelated pictures#Incorrect. A collage may be visual, but unrelated pictures do not clarify changes over time.
~C. A background pattern with many colors#Incorrect. A pattern is decorative and does not explain sequence. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. A long paragraph copied onto a slide#Incorrect. A long paragraph may overwhelm the audience instead of improving understanding.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q04::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student says the purpose of a chart is "to make the presentation look smart."
Which response shows better thinking?{
~A. The chart should stay because charts always impress audiences.#Incorrect. A chart must help explain the content, not simply impress viewers. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. The chart should be replaced with a funny image.#Incorrect. Humor does not necessarily improve understanding. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. The chart should stay only if it helps the audience understand the evidence.#Correct. Multimedia should be included because it clarifies the message for the audience. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. The chart should be made harder to read so it looks advanced.#Incorrect. Difficult visuals weaken audience understanding. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q05::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A slide has six small images of buildings, roads, and people, but no caption or speaker note identifies which image supports the claim.
What is the likely problem?{
~A. The slide has too little color#Incorrect. The main issue is not color; it is the lack of purpose and explanation. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. The audience may not know which image supports the claim#Correct. Too many unexplained images can confuse the audience and weaken the presentation. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. The slide should include no visuals ever#Incorrect. Visuals can be useful when they support the message. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. The speaker should remove all words from every slide#Incorrect. Clear labels and captions often help audience understanding. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q06::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student chooses a map for a presentation about a journey in a text.
What caption would best support the audience?{
=A. This map shows the route that shapes the character's major decisions.#Correct. The caption connects the map to the presentation's analysis. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. This is a map I found online.#Incorrect. This does not explain how the map supports audience understanding. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Maps are useful in many classes.#Incorrect. This is general and does not connect to the specific presentation. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Look at this picture closely.#Incorrect. The audience needs a purposeful explanation, not a vague command. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q07::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student wants to use academic vocabulary while explaining a visual.
Which explanation is strongest?{
~A. This picture is nice and makes the slide better.#Incorrect. This explanation is vague and does not use precise academic language. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. This image is cool because it looks dramatic.#Incorrect. This focuses on appearance instead of analysis or audience understanding. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. This visual has things that connect to stuff in the story.#Incorrect. The language is too vague for a clear presentation. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. This image clarifies the historical context that influences the character's conflict.#Correct. The explanation uses precise academic vocabulary and connects the visual to analysis. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q08::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A presentation includes a source note under an image.
Why is the source note useful?{
~A. It makes the image look smaller#Incorrect. A source note is not mainly about size or design. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. It shows where the image came from and supports responsible presentation practice#Correct. Source notes help audiences understand and trust the use of multimedia. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. It replaces the need to explain the image#Incorrect. A source note does not replace analysis or explanation. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. It lets the speaker skip the claim#Incorrect. The claim is still necessary for a clear presentation. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q09::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student adds background music during a serious explanation.
When would the music be appropriate?{
~A. When it is loud enough to cover the speaker#Incorrect. Music that covers the speaker harms audience understanding. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. When it is brief, purposeful, and directly supports the audience's understanding#Correct. Multimedia should be controlled and tied to the message. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. When the student likes the song#Incorrect. Personal preference does not make multimedia useful for the audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. When the music has no connection to the topic#Incorrect. Unrelated music distracts from the presentation. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q10::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student creates a digital presentation for a research claim.
Which question best checks multimedia quality?{
=A. Does this multimedia help the audience understand my claim or evidence?#Correct. This question focuses on audience understanding and purpose. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. Does this multimedia make my presentation longer?#Incorrect. Length is not a measure of quality. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Does this multimedia use the brightest color?#Incorrect. Bright color does not guarantee clarity or relevance. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Does this multimedia replace my explanation?#Incorrect. Multimedia should support explanation, not replace it. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q11::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A slide graph compares three sources, but the labels are too small and only one data point connects to the claim.
What revision best improves audience experience?{
~A. Keep the graph tiny so all details fit#Incorrect. Tiny labels make information hard to read. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Remove the graph and add clip art#Incorrect. Clip art may not support the research. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. Simplify the graph and label only the details connected to the claim#Correct. Simplifying and labeling relevant details helps the audience understand the evidence. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. Add a long paragraph beside the graph#Incorrect. Too much text can overload the slide. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q12::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student says, "My image shows the main idea."
What should the student add?{
~A. A second image with no caption#Incorrect. More images do not fix an unclear explanation. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. A brighter border#Incorrect. A border does not explain the image's connection to the main idea. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. A sound effect#Incorrect. A sound effect is unlikely to clarify the analytical connection. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. A specific explanation of how the image supports the main idea#Correct. The audience needs to know how the multimedia connects to the claim. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q13::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A slide includes a quote and an image.
Which arrangement best supports understanding?{
~A. Put the quote and image anywhere there is space#Incorrect. Placement should help the audience see the connection. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Place the image near the quote and explain how both support the point#Correct. Proximity and explanation help the audience connect media, evidence, and analysis. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. Hide the quote in small text below the image#Incorrect. Important evidence should be readable. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Use the image only and remove the explanation#Incorrect. The speaker still needs to explain the connection to the claim. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q14::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A presentation uses a video clip.
What is the best reason to include it?{
=A. The clip demonstrates a key concept that the audience may not understand from text alone.#Correct. Video is useful when it clarifies a concept for the audience. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. The clip is longer than the presentation notes.#Incorrect. Length does not make a clip useful. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The clip is popular online.#Incorrect. Popularity is not the same as relevance. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. The clip gives the speaker a break from talking.#Incorrect. Multimedia should support communication, not replace responsibility for explanation.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q15::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student is choosing between two images.
Which image is the better choice?{
~A. The image with the most dramatic colors, even if unrelated#Incorrect. Dramatic color does not matter if the image is unrelated. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. The image that fills the whole slide but has no clear purpose#Incorrect. Size does not create relevance. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The image the speaker likes most personally#Incorrect. Personal preference is not the main criterion. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. The image that most clearly supports the claim and can be explained in a caption#Correct. Relevance and explainability make the image useful for the audience. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q16::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student includes a table of research findings.
Which change would help the audience most?{
~A. Add every number from the original source#Incorrect. Too many numbers can overwhelm the audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Highlight the findings that directly support the presentation claim#Correct. Highlighting relevant findings helps the audience focus on the evidence that matters. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. Remove the title and labels#Incorrect. Titles and labels help the audience understand the table. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Use smaller font so more data fits#Incorrect. Smaller font may make the table unreadable. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q17::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student wants multimedia to improve a presentation.
Which plan is strongest?{
=A. Use one relevant visual, label it clearly, and explain its connection to the claim.#Correct. This plan uses multimedia purposefully and supports audience understanding. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. Use as many visuals as possible so the audience stays busy.#Incorrect. Too many visuals can distract or overload the audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Use visuals only on the title slide.#Incorrect. Multimedia should appear where it supports understanding. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Use visuals without labels so the slides look clean.#Incorrect. Missing labels can make multimedia confusing. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q18::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student writes a caption with several spelling errors.
Why should the caption be revised?{
~A. Captions should be removed from all presentations#Incorrect. Captions are useful when they explain multimedia. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Spelling only matters in essays#Incorrect. Conventions matter in presentation materials too. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. Errors can distract the audience and weaken the credibility of the presentation#Correct. Correct conventions support clarity and audience trust. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. Errors make the slide more casual and friendly#Incorrect. Errors usually reduce quality and clarity. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q19::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student uses a chart that contradicts the spoken claim.
What should the student do?{
~A. Keep the chart because visuals are always helpful#Incorrect. A contradictory chart confuses the audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Ignore the contradiction and speak quickly#Incorrect. Avoiding the problem does not improve understanding. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Make the chart smaller so no one notices#Incorrect. Hiding the issue does not solve the problem. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. Revise the claim or replace the chart so the multimedia accurately supports the message#Correct. Multimedia must align accurately with the presentation's claim and evidence. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q20::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A slide has a strong image but no connection to the evidence.
Which speaker note best fixes the problem?{
~A. This image is here because I needed another slide.#Incorrect. This shows the image is not purposeful. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. This image helps show the environment described in the evidence, which explains the character's decision.#Correct. This note connects the image to evidence and analysis. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. This image is pretty and makes the presentation better.#Incorrect. This focuses on appearance instead of understanding. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. This image does not need explanation.#Incorrect. Audiences often need explanation to connect multimedia to the claim. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q21::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student is designing slides for an audience unfamiliar with the topic.
Which choice best supports that audience?{
=A. Include brief labels and visuals that define key ideas before analysis begins.#Correct. An unfamiliar audience needs context before deeper analysis. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. Use unexplained expert terms to sound advanced.#Incorrect. Unexplained terms may confuse the audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Skip background information entirely.#Incorrect. Without background, the audience may not understand the analysis. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Use only decorative media.#Incorrect. Decoration does not build audience understanding. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q22::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student uses a diagram in a presentation.
What makes the diagram effective?{
~A. It includes every detail from the student's notes#Incorrect. Too many details can make a diagram hard to understand. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. It is unrelated but visually interesting#Incorrect. Interest is not enough if the diagram does not support the message. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. It replaces all spoken explanation#Incorrect. A diagram should support explanation, not eliminate it. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. It organizes complex information so the audience can follow the relationship between ideas#Correct. Effective diagrams make relationships easier to understand. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q23::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student is unsure whether to include an audio clip.
Which test should the student use?{
~A. Does it make the presentation feel longer?#Incorrect. Length does not show quality. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Can I explain how it improves the audience's understanding of my point?#Correct. If the student can connect the clip to understanding, it may be useful. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. Do I personally like listening to it?#Incorrect. Personal preference is not enough. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Will it surprise the audience even if unrelated?#Incorrect. Surprise without relevance can distract. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q24::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student has a slide with a quote, a chart, two images, and a video link.
What is the best revision?{
~A. Add another image so the slide looks balanced#Incorrect. More media may increase overload. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Remove the quote because visuals are always better#Incorrect. Evidence may be necessary, so removal should not be automatic. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. Choose the multimedia element that best clarifies the point and move extra material elsewhere#Correct. Selecting the most useful media reduces overload and improves audience understanding. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. Keep everything because all information is helpful#Incorrect. Too much information on one slide can confuse the audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q25::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student revises a multimedia presentation before submitting.
Which final check best matches the lesson standard?{
=A. Each multimedia element improves audience understanding of the claim, evidence, or research.#Correct. This directly checks whether the presentation uses multimedia to improve the audience experience. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. Each slide has at least three colors.#Incorrect. Color count does not measure audience understanding. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Each slide has the same number of pictures.#Incorrect. Equal picture count does not matter if the media is not purposeful. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Each image is larger than the text.#Incorrect. Size alone does not make multimedia effective. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}
